Lunch break. You reread some notes for later. A report, a lesson, a recipe. You feel like you've got it. More than a hundred years ago, one researcher tested his own memory in a strange way. He learned long lists of nonsense syllables, then measured how much work it took to learn them again after different delays. If relearning was fast, the memory was still partly there. If it was slow, it was mostly gone. Not long ago, two researchers repeated this experiment. One of them, a young researcher, spent about seventy hours over seventy-five days learning and relearning lists. The pattern came back almost the same as before. Twenty minutes after learning a list, relearning it already took about half the original effort. After one day, about two-thirds. After a month, almost the whole effort again. This is the forgetting curve. Most of the loss happens early, in the first hours. Then it slows down. That sounds bad. But think about what it means. Your brain doesn't treat everything you see as worth keeping. It waits for proof. Something you meet once and never use again fades fast. Something that keeps coming back gets kept. That's a smart rule for a brain that sees thousands of things a day. Most of them really don't matter. And here is where most of us get it wrong. We try to prove something matters by rereading it. But rereading is a weak signal. The words look familiar, so it feels like you know them. Your brain is mostly recognizing the page, not storing anything new. There's a much stronger signal, and we'll come back to it at the end, because it's the one thing in this video you can use today. First, let's get to the evening. Because the strangest part of memory isn't what you forget. It's what you remember that isn't true.[0m
